{"id":"h239","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"The units for the rate of a chemical reaction are\nIs the correct answer \"it depends on the particular reaction\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The rate of a chemical reaction has units of concentration per time, typically mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.9,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹ and mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹ are equivalent units.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The units of the rate constant k depend on the reaction order.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.3,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Rates can be defined per unit surface area or in terms of amount, which changes the units.","confidence":0.6,"lean":0.4,"theta":0.9425,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The standard definition of rate (change in concentration over time) gives mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹ regardless of stoichiometry or order.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.9,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The reaction-dependent variation applies to the rate constant, not to the rate itself.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"A zeroth-order reaction has a rate constant in M s⁻¹ and a rate in M s⁻¹.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-0.5,"theta":1.3352,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Surface-area or volume-based rate definitions are special conventions rather than the general answer.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-0.6,"theta":1.2566,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The correct answer is mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹, not 'it depends on the particular reaction'.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":8,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":4,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},